#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105659 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdb609d-4000-5ee3-9267-71ed6a8dd140" :question/math-academy-id "e-7392" :question/problem "It is known that $75\\%$ of students pass a particular exam. If $5$ students who took the exam are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ of them passed the exam? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105660 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "cbc5bcb5-2c64-5895-ba1c-cc6d86207667" :question/math-academy-id "e-7393" :question/problem "A standard fair die is rolled $8$ times. What is the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.5)^{7}(0.5)+1(0.5)^{8} \\\\  & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105661 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "1fab59f6-f640-5533-9bff-4946dfd71f77" :question/math-academy-id "e-7394" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ college professors\n\n- The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n\n- The heights of $10$ elementary school children" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105664 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "b4cfa71b-69f8-5cdb-b3c9-6dabdb32e264" :question/math-academy-id "e-7444" :question/problem "In a random card game, there is a $40\\%$ chance of winning a game. If the player plays $12$ games, what is the probability that he wins at least $2$ of the games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=40\\%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.4).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player wins at least 2 of the games. This corresponds to $P(X \\geq 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \\ldots + P(X=12)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{12-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{12-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{12}+12(0.4)(0.6)^{11}] \\\\  & =1-0.019\\,591 \\\\  & =0.980\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411499} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd34f00-e35e-5bc2-9e2e-d848768a619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411513} {:answer/id #uuid "c72a5b0e-4bd0-5a33-aae9-d61e9b22e3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411520} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e7ec7-8b44-5e18-b5c4-bed1de80a167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411524} {:answer/id #uuid "f3648a58-4da3-5f65-8f21-ee1b6cdfadce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411499} :answer-field/id #uuid "b34d899f-07f1-5450-a677-a95d2bf81727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29a87319-221e-52be-a49a-7a7f42765bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130357" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam in a class of $25$ students\n- The number of cards drawn until a red card is obtained\n- The number of books published by a publishing house in a month" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $25$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although drawing a red card can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of books published cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411476} {:answer/id #uuid "291531dd-6f7e-59b2-9b00-3a0fc4cc8ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411482} {:answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411495} {:answer/id #uuid "970d7ed7-c547-5138-9f77-c9dd3621443a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411512} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c5e69a-ff05-5da9-a145-db810e12de50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411495} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e06f09da-f1cd-5f64-925a-f4c6e513e373" :question/math-academy-id "q-130361" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of trees in a forest of $300$ trees that have bird's nests\n- The number of vaccines administered in a country during one season\n- The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of trees that have bird's nests among $300$ trees can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $300$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although receiving a vaccine can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177c5f24-3832-5fee-961f-92a28b65ff5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.173" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411473} {:answer/id #uuid "30a08fd6-9eca-5ed7-96b9-1e94fa413565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.989" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411485} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3ef3da-4f6a-57f3-b072-97ce1d7304c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411510} {:answer/id #uuid "d4345274-9a8a-5da8-9701-5446ba840e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.272" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411523} {:answer/id #uuid "e1db3c8b-0f51-5da7-b9c0-02c9c8fda687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1db3c8b-0f51-5da7-b9c0-02c9c8fda687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e65c8161-0653-52f7-980c-c7f61d6aeb33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69ccd3d4-6bc6-5b97-931d-42636fcb0b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-130748" :question/problem "$70%$ of union members are in favor of a specific change to their conditions of employment. A random sample of $6$ members is taken. What is the probability that at most $2$ members are in favor of the changed conditions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of members in the sample in favor of the change.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ members\n\n- since $70%$ of union members are in favor of the change, the probability of selecting a member in favor of the change is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(1-0.7)}^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(0.3)}^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most two members are in favor of the changed conditions. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}){(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}){(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{6-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{6-2} \\\\  & =1{(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6}+6{(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{5}+15{(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.070\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.998" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411468} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfd0d54-03a3-56e8-826f-a80849a9aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.009" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411469} {:answer/id #uuid "38fd8a19-3d7c-53ef-8685-0b3fe7865ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.889" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411489} {:answer/id #uuid "62e8c490-2c01-565b-af6a-a26106979fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411501} {:answer/id #uuid "f73ed5db-8aa0-5ab5-9fa9-5db0867880fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.938" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.998" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411468} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ae2aad-9d53-58ac-93a0-f36b60a4bfa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d707ab1-b369-599a-9705-58f642e1bb10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130880" :question/problem "An infectious flu virus is spreading through a school. The probability of a randomly selected student having the flu next week is $0.3.$ If a class has $25$ students, what is the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of students having the flu next week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=25$ students in the class\n\n- the probability of selecting a student having the flu next week is $0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(25,0.3).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(1-0.3)^{25-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{25-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=25)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{25-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25}+25(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{24}] \\\\  & =1-0.001\\,571 \\\\  & ≈0.998\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411470} {:answer/id #uuid "16e1f5c3-3735-584e-8765-6dea06b02e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411472} {:answer/id #uuid "6412fbb1-01e9-5e4f-95c5-7d8cf906c60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411502} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1a31e0-ee07-527f-ba69-44baa27e2377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.967" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411506} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8368ff-4749-5812-ba39-ab317a2cc469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411470} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c7a716-fc4c-5004-8bf8-cfd3d0945b59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1efc3f5-91c0-5d80-b7bf-74abaff81c0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130882" :question/problem "$5%$ of electric bulbs are defective at manufacture. If six bulbs are randomly tested, with each one being replaced before the next is chosen, what is the probability that at least two are defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of defective tested bulbs. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ tested bulbs\n\n- the probability of choosing a defective bulb is $p=5%=0.05$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.05).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(1-0.05)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(0.95)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least two bulbs are defective. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{6-1}] \\\\  & =1-[1(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6}+6(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.967\\,226 \\\\  & ≈0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3383f195-912b-5799-ad90-e9e9dc9bee9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411487} {:answer/id #uuid "33f90eab-98a8-56e1-ba68-6b050ad4df9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411488} {:answer/id #uuid "72382a44-6c7d-56b0-818d-1f68163d43f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.927" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411507} {:answer/id #uuid "bf599ee1-76c6-5863-8598-76bbd4c27c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411518} {:answer/id #uuid "f46341a5-5f4f-578d-831f-6f6149790cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3383f195-912b-5799-ad90-e9e9dc9bee9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411487} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d275997-d198-54d6-a316-0c412fd339be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfd01f57-c0cd-576d-bb6c-c4a4c9533c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131048" :question/problem "A box of chocolates contains cherry creams and almond little cakes in the ratio of $3:2.$ Ten chocolates are selected at random, with replacement. What is the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $3:2,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{3}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{3}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 9).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 9)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=9)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =1-P(X>9) \\\\  & =1-P(X=10) \\\\  & =1-(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-10} \\\\  & =1-1{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0} \\\\  & =1-0.006\\,047 \\\\  & ≈0.994\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411474} :answer-field/id #uuid "1404ccfc-3152-5913-b96d-4cc109f39c7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48a19d94-de64-5ea0-b742-40601b9c76a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331491" :question/problem "A bag contains $5$ green marbles, $7$ yellow marbles, and $8$ black marbles. An experiment of randomly selecting one marble from the bag and putting it back is repeated $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a black marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a black marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ selections.\n\n- The probability of selecting a black marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{5+7+8}=\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.276,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4da32402-ac89-5a1b-a640-f9d556346571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4da32402-ac89-5a1b-a640-f9d556346571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411494} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72832e-c3d2-5add-a583-b41863a1a684" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c93c3864-ab3f-5794-a142-3eab6e62d56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331492" :question/problem "A bag contains $9$ blue marbles, $5$ red marbles, and $6$ green marbles. A marble is drawn at random and replaced before the next draw. This process is repeated $7$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a blue marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a blue marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ trials.\n\n- The probability of selecting a blue marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{9+5+6}=\\frac{9}{20}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,\\frac{9}{20}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3}){(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{9}{20})}^{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!}{(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{9}{20})}^{7-3} \\\\  & =35{(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(\\frac{11}{20})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.292,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411475} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dd545c-98d8-56db-ace2-b43058497897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec42c1b6-69ea-5d9d-b8b5-5ad8c82e5789" :question/math-academy-id "q-331525" :question/problem "A basketball player has a $50%$ chance of making a free throw. If they take $7$ shots, what is the probability that they make at most $3$ of them?\n\nThe probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ shots.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=50%=0.5.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.5).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $3$ or fewer of the $7$ shots are successful. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & ={\\sum }_{x=0}^{3}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})](0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =(1+7+21+35)\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b2e490a-1bb1-5990-9e77-13fbe35019de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.572" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b2e490a-1bb1-5990-9e77-13fbe35019de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.572" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411505} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a882d2-167d-5383-9684-88e207eea076" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a48cf853-bbc5-5226-99de-0617e7d6b622" :question/math-academy-id "q-331962" :question/problem "A drone has a $65%$ chance of successfully delivering a package. It makes $5$ deliveries in one day. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it succeeds at most $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful deliveries.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ deliveries.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=65%=0.65.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.65).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(1-0.65)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(0.35)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the drone succeeds at most $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.65)^{4}(0.35)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.65)^{5}(0.35)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.65)^{4}(0.35)+(0.65)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.428\\,415 \\\\  & ≈0.572,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64357ced-be65-5f92-896c-49a39f578379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411503} {:answer/id #uuid "692b3019-71a9-5b63-9e3c-abdf054e7e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411504} {:answer/id #uuid "855beeb5-4f39-591a-a8ed-3abb6b72c857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411509} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb328de-2e12-51ae-a30e-e7eea7510405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.688" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411521} {:answer/id #uuid "db8a2a09-b8f1-5b4e-9e51-08645ed163c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.092" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "692b3019-71a9-5b63-9e3c-abdf054e7e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411504} :answer-field/id #uuid "3887d38e-9181-547d-8de9-9f160529041d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bd6c9ff-00f9-5185-b985-9d9007df87dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84050" :question/problem "A softball team has a $70%$ chance of winning each game in a $10$-game season. What is the probability that the team wins $9$ of the $10$ games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ We want to find the probability of winning $x=9$ games.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(1-0.7)^{10-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{9!(10-9)!}(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3) \\\\  & =0.121\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411477} {:answer/id #uuid "4a98ac69-4946-50af-be1a-a87eda63d1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411493} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae6abc-b491-5c69-9fda-9c44145f964f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411497} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3a5183-df01-521a-be7e-92566b8b266a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411528} {:answer/id #uuid "de49179b-7a0c-5bcd-ab74-23a6fdd2bd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411477} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b85b9db-4b3a-5931-a044-422d92ddf7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dd54609-ae0c-53a9-a2a1-7823f9aa6595" :question/math-academy-id "q-84747" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of times \"head\" is obtained when a coin is tossed $10$ times\n- The number of students in a class\n- The number of tickets sold for a concert in $3$ days" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of \"heads\" in $10$ tosses can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students in a class cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of tickets sold for a concert cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c0fe61-4cd5-5060-819b-ff762f83aae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411492} {:answer/id #uuid "500babc9-c423-5d42-a392-144709fe6b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411496} {:answer/id #uuid "5481292b-246a-5d52-93ec-b37d3cc2c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411498} {:answer/id #uuid "e849fced-d809-50ad-a2d4-a3c41ddca395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411531} {:answer/id #uuid "f93fce79-3e49-557b-96a5-c10f504fd798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93fce79-3e49-557b-96a5-c10f504fd798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411540} :answer-field/id #uuid "d22af52a-6cb4-5c75-ae52-e745c13307b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46c22425-ee4e-5a56-9d8f-8f1ffa9ada86" :question/math-academy-id "q-84748" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ soccer players\n- The price of a pair of pants in a department store\n- The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ff577d-7f26-5b83-b643-9fce2c24c06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.618" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411478} {:answer/id #uuid "26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.655" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411481} {:answer/id #uuid "7cddf7d6-cafe-5fe4-969c-5fecde39057e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.715" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411508} {:answer/id #uuid "d8312f94-a2cf-5afb-8d33-ce7a63d7d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411525} {:answer/id #uuid "dad7de5f-fb75-59c7-891d-0b4f384f052d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.737" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.655" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411481} :answer-field/id #uuid "b50d0c55-e310-5f1f-ba20-584d4a9a1df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0df1382-8e7a-5174-b609-3e041c2744d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84797" :question/problem "On a particular webpage, there is an $80%$ probability that each downloaded file does not contain a computer virus. If $6$ files are downloaded, what is the probability that at least $5$ of the files are virus-free? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of virus-free files.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ files\n\n- the probability that each file is virus-free is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $5$ of the $6$ files are virus-free. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.8)^{5}(0.2)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.8)^{5}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & =0.655\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ca9df2e-63f3-5b2f-8b41-7f7f2f9a6582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.974" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411490} {:answer/id #uuid "5d36912d-c8ee-5759-a4ba-72a04e64cba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.026" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411500} {:answer/id #uuid "ad502918-2f4f-5ee1-ab53-423479807ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.871" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411515} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6149b3-e75f-58c2-b376-432afa2a115b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.996" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411533} {:answer/id #uuid "ec808eb3-43a1-5e60-8d02-f99e9dec48f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.897" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca9df2e-63f3-5b2f-8b41-7f7f2f9a6582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.974" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411490} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e2700b-68a3-5d33-878c-99b41a013f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a3cf3a8-b182-51ac-8e52-79ae8f41f3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-84807" :question/problem "In a bag containing candies, there is a $70%$ chance of getting a red candy when a random candy is taken. If $16$ candies are removed from the bag, what is the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\nYou may assume that the bag contains a large number of candies and that the probability of selecting a red candy is the same on each trial." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of red candies.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ candies\n\n- the probability of getting a red candy is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red. This corresponds to $P(X<15).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X<15)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<15) & =1-P(X\\ge 15) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=15)+P(X=16)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{15})(0.7)^{15}(0.3)^{16-15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{16})(0.7)^{16}(0.3)^{16-16}] \\\\  & =1-[16(0.7)^{15}(0.3)+(0.7)^{16}] \\\\  & =1-0.026\\,112 \\\\  & =0.974\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc945cd-e0a6-5e86-adc0-7a5446b2a41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355050} {:answer/id #uuid "946e3569-88e7-5e1b-88fa-a93871220bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355971} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d1e7c0-f30e-5e03-ba20-ced2f5de00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} {:answer/id #uuid "d39b7d0b-0005-5346-9793-d0d086cf68b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356513} {:answer/id #uuid "f03bc8d1-6109-574a-817b-84faa4cb038b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1d1e7c0-f30e-5e03-ba20-ced2f5de00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6b99f6-a1a6-5460-bc79-7d630213eba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3640d184-7917-5cae-8823-08846078aa32" :question/math-academy-id "q-89354" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$\n- The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine\n- The number of views of a video on a popular video channel" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of baskets in $3$ throws can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $100$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of views cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc28274-a70b-5d5e-bb19-cf673ed6833a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355341} {:answer/id #uuid "4e445193-6d9a-5bd8-850e-4bcde5fe10bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355358} {:answer/id #uuid "697a72b6-5f3d-5c77-9de6-ab9f53145994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.089" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355593} {:answer/id #uuid "a57fb1fa-437e-53a3-8f89-114d427fe1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356132} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e754f5-15e5-5c3d-9939-e1d2372af236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a57fb1fa-437e-53a3-8f89-114d427fe1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356132} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad9ad01-2ce3-5873-a99b-0a674040b68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16573e53-10fd-55fd-9ec2-393558355143" :question/math-academy-id "q-89366" :question/problem "A spinner with three regions is designed so that the probability it lands on red is $\\frac{3}{10},$ the probability it lands on blue is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and on green $\\frac{3}{10}.$ What is the probability that the spinner lands on red or green exactly $5$ times if it is spun $12$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on red or green.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ spins\n\n- the probability of landing on red or green on any spin is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{3}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{5}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{12-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{5!(12-5)!}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =792{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fce3391-052e-587a-a9de-fd2fea484deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.097" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411484} {:answer/id #uuid "3301780f-7d13-59bf-babc-3915b8c2d051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411486} {:answer/id #uuid "90225c0b-d22b-5ec7-82e0-736f61864ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411511} {:answer/id #uuid "e928e1f5-d998-5632-b572-4eff4fc046da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.217" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411532} {:answer/id #uuid "eff5311f-f548-5e70-829c-1a093a0352eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.167" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eff5311f-f548-5e70-829c-1a093a0352eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.167" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411536} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e2ae69-22b8-5724-b660-3eef8661b061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e0e75d6-02e9-556c-b809-4824d53487ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-89380" :question/problem "A fair $5$-sided die with numbers $1$ through $5$ is rolled $9$ times. What is the probability of getting a $1$ or a $2$ exactly five times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $1$ or a $2$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=9$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a $1$ or $2$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(9,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{5}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{9-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!(9-5)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =126{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =0.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13359ee6-0e60-5eae-8685-260358b6d02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.397" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411471} {:answer/id #uuid "24186a4f-baf5-5bbf-8437-a6d633e7ff67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.317" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411479} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbaf130-f38c-5cec-b719-9fe23cda6746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.376" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411483} {:answer/id #uuid "b09d1f6a-90b7-5eb8-a6f9-4d7a285a4280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.345" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411517} {:answer/id #uuid "fec98c8f-bd15-5730-a9c2-11f482fcd350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbaf130-f38c-5cec-b719-9fe23cda6746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.376" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411483} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6dbfdb-46b1-5bb4-ba87-2a3e95b166df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90500394-6fc0-5ba4-a5dc-ea52dd5425de" :question/math-academy-id "q-89398" :question/problem "A basketball player practices shooting $10$ free throws. The player has an $80%$ chance of making a basket in each free throw. What is the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of baskets that the player makes.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ free throws\n\n- the probability of making a basket in each free throw is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.8)^{9}(0.2)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.8)^{10}(0.2)^{0} \\\\  & =10(0.8)^{9}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{10} \\\\  & =0.376\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247eb43d-feb9-5cee-9894-cb5032551bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.318" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411480} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea6bbf8-7531-55c5-bbc3-cab16fbb769f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.358" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411491} {:answer/id #uuid "a2efb8b4-7301-5268-9953-cc95879d588d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.385" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411514} {:answer/id #uuid "af3fd1a3-b502-54c5-8842-d31fc8bf7413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411516} {:answer/id #uuid "c6121925-4323-53df-85ac-9e96651a8679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ea6bbf8-7531-55c5-bbc3-cab16fbb769f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.358" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411491} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3d9610-1715-554d-8262-9b8d134a3c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "265e293f-7554-5958-abfd-df783c671c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-89399" :question/problem "It is known that $41%$ of a given population have blood type O+. What is the probability that at most $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type O+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people with blood type O+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ trials\n\n- the probability of finding a person with blood type O+ is\n\n$$\np=0.41\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.41).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(1-0.41)}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(0.59)}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a person with blood type O+) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0}){(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1}){(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2}){(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3}){(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & ={(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+10{(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+45{(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+120{(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & =0.358\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
